  {
   "op": "note",
   "uid": "t-door-w",
   "text": "Walk 12: the owner saw the two porticos as free-standing doors into nothing. Each now stands at the foot of a hill that climbs from the path (m-a-hill-w, m-a-hill-e, ramp profile, soft blend) and closes on the portico's back over its lintel (m-d-back-w, m-d-back-e); a forecourt is dug to the sill with sloped sides (m-b-cut-w, m-b-cut-e) and a ramp leads down into it from the path (m-c-ramp-w, m-c-ramp-e). Part 3: mound uids carry a/b/c/d because overlays apply in id order (hill, cut, ramp, back). The scrubbing scaffolds are gone (owner, walk 12): each portico is itself the parcel's host, a door-only shell carrying the plugin load door into the temple."
  },
  {
   "op": "note",
   "uid": "t-rock-en1",
   "text": "Walk 12 close-out: the east portico's north side faces the street with no bank behind it; a bank rock closes that side so the door reads as cut into the hill."
  },
  {
   "op": "note",
   "uid": "t-tooth3",
   "text": "Walk 12: the owner saw no row of teeth. The row is drawn 4 to 6 m nearer the bank, every tooth in knee-deep water, at 1.35 to 1.65 times the mined scale, each turned differently, so the six teeth read as a weathered colonnade from the landing rather than a set of identical posts (close-out)."
  },
  {
   "op": "note",
   "uid": "t-rock-wn1",
   "text": "Walk 12 close-out: two bank rocks flank the west portico so its door reads as cut into the rock of the hill; the hill ramps start 2 to 3 m behind the forecourt so its sides are low banks, not pit walls."
  },
  {
   "op": "note",
   "uid": "t-tooth1",
   "text": "The Teeth of Sithis: six black xanmeer teeth standing in knee-deep water off the bank, the pilgrim way running out at the water opposite the gap between the middle pair. Two doors in the rock of the bank lead down into the temple below."
  },
